Z||000000||FieroTig||05-10-2001||11:42 PM||||i can't figure this out, i have looked at all over the car. my 85 gt keeps blowing this fuse, it's the fuse for the parking lights.. i have ruled out the head light relays, because the lights still go down when you put a fresh fuse in, as long as the parking lights are out. i have followed all the electrcal lines and i don't see a short. is there any places i should pay special atention? and is there anything i can use as a fuse that won't keep blowing everytime i think i found it? any help would be apreciated. thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||05-11-2001||07:48 AM||||Did you check the connections at the switch?  What about wires under the dash, maybe something rubbing against something else.  Also check the firewall connector that is near the blower motor in the front compartment, where the wires run through from the compartment to the interior.<P>Did you check the bulb sockets themselves?  Take out the lense and have a look inside them.  Also, once you have removed the lenses, take out the bulbs, turn the lights on, and then turn them off and see if your fuse still blows.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||05-11-2001||12:50 PM||||Has it ever been bumped in the front hard? because there is a bundle of wires that gets pinched when the frame members bend.. It's hard to see them because they run in font of the radiator along the top crossmember||||reg||3
Z||000003||thomas_l||05-11-2001||02:06 PM||||Most decent tool dealers (I think NAPA has them too) sell a short finder. Basically you get a +/-10A circuit breaker and a small inductive ammeter. You plug the breaker into the fuse socket that keeps blowing. It starts clicking like a turn signal as it overloads, opens, then resets. You follow the wiring with the ammeter which will "twitch" every time the breaker cycles. When you pass the location of the short, the needle stops moving with the breaker. The tool will work thru trim and moulding (no need to remove sill plates or cut up carpeting). <P>Of course you waste time with that after looking for obvious stuff like pinched wiring harness and accessory mounting screws (like for new dash speakers, extra gauges, alarms, fog lights, etc.) that found a fat harness to dig into  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
